+# Model card — `delay_risk_logreg_booking`
+
+> Generated by `python -m ml.model_card` from `artifacts/model/model_metadata.json`. Do not edit by hand.
+
+| | |
+|---|---|
+| Version | `20261008163630` (trained 2026-10-08T16:36:30+00:00) |
+| Task | At booking time, predict whether a shipment will arrive **more than 24 h late** |
+| Target | `actual_delay_hours > 24`, positive rate 40.5% |
+| Training data | 4,564 labelled shipments, bookings 2024-06-01 → 2025-10-14 |
+| Split | temporal by booking_date; dev = oldest 85% (rolling-origin CV, 4 expanding folds), test = newest 15% (evaluated once) |
+| Selected model | `logreg_booking` — hgb_booking beats logreg_booking by only 0.0112 mean CV PR-AUC (≤ 1 std = 0.0212); keeping the simpler, more explainable logreg_booking. |
+| Decision threshold | 0.5 (class-balanced model) |
+| Artefact | `delay_model.joblib`, sha256 `804c8006d6f2ce05…`, scikit-learn 1.6.1 |
+
+## Features (all known at booking time)
+
+| Feature | Why it is included |
+|---|---|
+| `origin_port` | Port-specific operations (congestion, labour, weather exposure). |
+| `destination_port` | Same, at the discharge end — where an arrival delay is actually measured. |
+| `cargo_type` | Inspection-heavy cargo (pharma, chemicals, food) can be held at customs. |
+| `origin_region` | Coarser version of origin_port; helps ports with few shipments. |
+| `destination_region` | Coarser version of destination_port. |
+| `region_lane` | Trade lane (e.g. APAC->EUR): route-level effects without 600 sparse route dummies. |
+| `container_count` | Larger consignments need more handling slots. |
+| `weight_tons` | Heavy cargo can be rolled to a later vessel when capacity is tight. |
+| `booking_lead_days` | Late bookings get worse slots; long lead times give planners slack. |
+| `transit_days_planned` | Longer voyages have more opportunity to accumulate delay. |
+| `origin_congestion` | Reference congestion score of the origin port (ports.csv). |
+| `destination_congestion` | Reference congestion score of the destination port. |
+| `departure_month` | Seasonality (peak season, monsoon, winter storms). |
+| `departure_dayofweek` | Weekend departures can face reduced port staffing. |
+| `booking_dayofweek` | Proxy for booking process / channel. |
+
+**Deliberately excluded**
+
+| Field | Reason |
+|---|---|
+| `actual_departure / actual_arrival / actual_delay_hours / transit_days_actual` | Leakage: only known after the fact — the target is derived from them. |
+| `status / on_time_flag` | Leakage: derived from the outcome. |
+| `shipment_id` | An identifier, not a property of the shipment. |
+| `customer_id / vessel_id (raw)` | Hundreds of sparse ids → memorisation. Their point-in-time history was tested instead (ml/history_features.py) and gave no lift. |
+| `port_events (same window)` | Events during the voyage are not known at booking. Events before booking were tested as point-in-time features — no lift. |
+
+## Model selection — rolling-origin cross-validation on the development set
+
+| Candidate | Servable | ROC-AUC | PR-AUC | F1 | Brier |
+|---|---|---|---|---|---|
+| `prior_baseline` | yes | 0.500 ± 0.000 | 0.411 ± 0.021 | 0.000 ± 0.000 | 0.242 ± 0.004 |
+| `logreg_booking` | yes | 0.494 ± 0.018 | 0.408 ± 0.021 | 0.452 ± 0.023 | 0.259 ± 0.006 |
+| `hgb_booking` | yes | 0.502 ± 0.015 | 0.419 ± 0.011 | 0.481 ± 0.032 | 0.267 ± 0.011 |
+| `logreg_booking_plus_history` | no (needs feature store) | 0.486 ± 0.019 | 0.406 ± 0.023 | 0.448 ± 0.035 | 0.259 ± 0.005 |
+
+## Final evaluation — untouched test set (685 shipments, bookings from 2025-08-04)
+
+| Metric | Model | Always predict late | Prior (base rate) |
+|---|---|---|---|
+| precision | **0.3861** | 0.3971 | 0.0 |
+| recall | **0.4485** | 1.0 | 0.0 |
+| f1 | **0.415** | 0.5684 | 0.0 |
+| roc_auc | **0.4883** | 0.5 | 0.5 |
+| pr_auc | **0.4087** | 0.3971 | 0.3971 |
+| brier | **0.2521** | 0.6029 | 0.2395 |
+
+95% bootstrap CI — ROC-AUC **0.4883 [0.4462, 0.5329]**, PR-AUC 0.4087 [0.359, 0.4659] (base rate 0.3971).
+
+### Label-permutation sanity test
+
+The same CV was repeated 20 times with **shuffled labels**. Null-model AUC: mean 0.4972, 95th percentile 0.5135. Real model: 0.494 → **p = 0.5714**.
+
+### Threshold trade-off (test set)
+
+| Threshold | Precision | Recall | F1 | Flagged |
+|---|---|---|---|---|
+| 0.3 | 0.3971 | 1.0 | 0.5684 | 100% |
+| 0.35 | 0.3971 | 1.0 | 0.5684 | 100% |
+| 0.4 | 0.3933 | 0.9485 | 0.556 | 96% |
+| 0.45 | 0.3949 | 0.7463 | 0.5165 | 75% |
+| 0.5 | 0.3861 | 0.4485 | 0.415 | 46% |
+| 0.55 | 0.404 | 0.1471 | 0.2156 | 14% |
+| 0.6 | 0.55 | 0.0404 | 0.0753 | 3% |
+| 0.65 | 0.75 | 0.011 | 0.0217 | 1% |
+| 0.7 | 0.0 | 0.0 | 0.0 | 0% |
+
+### Calibration (test set)
+
+| Predicted bin | n | Mean predicted | Observed late rate |
+|---|---|---|---|
+| 0.3-0.4 | 29 | 0.385 | 0.4828 |
+| 0.4-0.5 | 340 | 0.4564 | 0.4 |
+| 0.5-0.6 | 296 | 0.5359 | 0.375 |
+| 0.6-0.7 | 20 | 0.6222 | 0.55 |
+
+## Interpretation and intended use
+
+- **The model has no detectable predictive signal on this dataset.** Test ROC-AUC's confidence interval contains 0.5 and the permutation test cannot distinguish it from models trained on random labels. Every candidate — including gradient boosting and leakage-safe history features — lands in the same place.
+- Profiling agrees: no booking-time field moves the late rate by more than a few points (see `docs/DATA_QUALITY.md` → `planned_transit_outlier_for_route`; same-lane transit times vary 3–35 days).
+- Probabilities are **not calibrated** (class-balanced training centres them on 0.5). Use the risk band as a *relative* signal at most; do not use the probability as a true likelihood.
+- **Not suitable for automated decisions.** It is shipped to prove the serving, monitoring and leakage boundaries end-to-end; the same pipeline will pick up signal as soon as better features exist.
+
+## Monitoring
+
+`python -m ml.monitor --from <date>` compares a window of bookings against the training reference stored in the metadata (PSI per feature and on predictions), measures live performance on shipments whose outcome is known, and prints a retrain decision. Triggers are documented in the README.
+
